land State (10-4-3, 8); Evansville (15-3-4, 6); Indiana (21-3-1, 9).', 'Midwest Top Teams: Eastern Illinois (12-3-5, 9); North Texas State (16-4-2, 9); St. Louis (9-7-2, 8); Southern Illinois-Edwardsville (15-4-1, 7).', 'Midwest-Far West Top Teams: Claremont-Mudd-School (13-3-3, 8); Colorado College (12-10-2, 9); MacMurray (13-5-1, 7); Wheaton (Illinois) (18-2-2, 6).']), BatchSummary(summary="The article discusses several changes and decisions related to college sports, particularly in Division II and ice hockey. It mentions that the NCAA's Championships Standards Committee has granted an appeal, allowing Division II institutions to elevate their programs to Division I if they find it easier to qualify with fewer participants. Robert J. Kopnisky, wrestling coach at the University of Missouri, suggests that the current participation ratios for Division II should be justified using the same arguments as before, as reducing the number of participants could negatively impact the quality of the championships. The article also covers changes in dimensions and markings for ice hockey rinks that will take effect for the 1983-84 season, and it mentions that the United States may be challenged in Olympic baseball competition, with the possibility of a dramatic upset by a team from a country like Cuba. Finally, it highlights the United States' strong performance at the Pan American Games, with American collegiate athletes playing significant roles in winning a record number of medals, including gold medals in various sports.", key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='The document contains information about NCAA championship dates and sites for various sports, as well as details about automatic qualification for conferences in different divisions. It also includes changes to future championship dates and sites. The document is from the NCAA News dated August 31, 1983.', key_facts=['NCAA championships for wrestling, basketball, skiing, fencing, softball, and other sports have been scheduled for specific dates and locations.', "Future championships for volleyball, fencing, softball, and men's basketball have been set for 1985 and 1986.", 'Automatic qualification for conferences in different divisions has been determined.', "Several championship dates have been changed, including the West regional for Division I women's basketball championships."]), BatchSummary(summary="Here is a summary of the football coaching changes and additions across various institutions and programs. The details are as follows:\n\n**Rutgers**\n- **Bob Cox** has been added to the staff at Rutgers. He has a 172-50 record as a head coach at Rutgers. He previously coached at Princeton.\n\n**Delaware State**\n- **John Dixon** has been appointed as the defensive coordinator.\n\n**Salem State**\n- **Paul Allen** has resigned from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute to enter private business. He had been on the staff for four years.\n- **Pete Carr** has been hired as a men's soccer assistant coach at Drew University.\n\n**Plymouth State**\n- **Janae Nell** has been named as the women's soccer assistant coach at Plymouth State.\n- **Ed Rebholt** has been named as the football coach at Dubuque.\n\n**Clark (Massachusetts)**\n- **Bonnie Foley** has been named as the women's basketball coach at Clark University.\n- **Wanda Szeremeta** has been named as the women's softball coach at Clark University.\n\n**Monmouth (New Jersey)**\n- **Joseph P. Slowinski** has been named as the swimming and water polo coach at Monmouth University.\n\n**Southeast Missouri State**\n- **Jack Streck** has been hired as the head coach at Southeast Missouri State. He coached Drury College to NAIA titles in 1981 and 1982.\n\n**St. Louis**\n- **Gary Hasenmuehller** has been named as the assistant coach at St. Louis.\n- **Greg Schofield** has been named as the defensive coordinator at St. Louis, and will also be an assistant football coach.\n\n**Other**\n- **Melissa Millett** has been hired at Appalachian State.\n- **Diane Katz** has been hired at Manhattanville College.\n- **John Dixon** has been named as the defensive coordinator at Monmouth University.\n- **Dan Lloyds** has been named as the head coach at Wallawarnton High School.\n- **Bill Harra** has been named as the head coach at Villanova University.\n- **Rick Giancola** has been named as the head coach at Montclair State University.\n- **Gerald Carr** has been named as the offensive line coach at Davidson College.\n- **Jim Kinder** has been named as the offensive backs coach at Davidson College.\n- **Gerry Schofield** has been named as the defensive coordinator at Davidson College.\n- **Dan Lloyds** has been named as the head coach at Villanova University.\n- **Stevie Njirgal** has been named as the offensive ends coach at Davidson College.\n- **Bill Campli** has been named as the wide receiver coach at Rutgers University.\n- **Mike Wade** has been promoted to full-time status as the wide receiver coach at Missouri University.\n- **John Rint** has been named as the defensive coordinator at Delaware State University.\n- **Ernie Altam** and **Doug Romano** have been named as the offensive line and defensive line coaches at Columbia University.\n- **Lynn Hailstock** has been named as the secondary coach at Cincinnati University.\n- **Willie Burden** has been named as the receivers coach at Ohio State University.\n- **Mike Poe** has been named as the head wrestling coach at Millikin University.\n- **Jack Runchey** and **Bernie Anderson** have been named as assistant coaches at Western Michigan University.\n- **Paul Allen** has resigned from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute to enter private business.\n- **Pete Carr** has been hired as a men's soccer assistant coach at Drew University.\n- **Janae Nell** has been named as the women's soccer assistant coach at Plymouth State University.\n- **John Munns** has been named as the head wrestling coach at George Washington University.\n- **Mike Poe** has been named as the head wrestling coach at Millikin University.\n- **Rick Warner** and **Wes Kubacki** have been named as assistant coaches at Hobart College.\n- **Jack Streck** has been hired as the head coach at Southeast Missouri State University.\n\nThese are the main changes and additions to the coaching staff across various institutions and programs. Each coach's role and the institution they are being hired to coach at are clearly stated.", key_facts=['Bob Cox has been added to the Rutgers staff with a 172-50 record as a head coach at Rutgers.', 'John Dixon has been appointed as the defensive coordinator at Delaware State.', 'Paul Allen has resigned from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute to enter private business.', "Pete Carr has been hired as a men's soccer assistant coach at Drew University.", "Janae Nell has been named as the women's soccer assistant coach at Plymouth State.", 'Ed Rebholt has been named as the football coach at Dubuque.', "Bonnie Foley has been named as the women's basketball coach at Clark University.", "Wanda Szeremeta has been named as the women's softball coach at Clark University.", 'Joseph P. Slowinski has been named as the swimming and water polo coach at Monmouth University.', 'Jack Streck has been hired as the head coach at Southeast Missouri State University.', 'Gary Hasenmuehller has been named as the assistant coach at St. Louis University.', 'Greg Schofield has been named as the defensive coordinator at St. Louis University, and will also be an assistant football coach.', 'Melissa Millett has been hired at Appalachian State University.', 'Diane Katz has been hired at Manhattanville College.', 'John Dixon has been named as the defensive coordinator at Monmouth University.', 'Dan Lloyd has been named as the head coach at Villanova University.', 'Bill Harra has been named as the head coach at Villanova University.', 'Rick Giancola has been named as the head coach at Montclair State University.', 'Gerald Carr has been named as the offensive line coach at Davidson College.', 'Jim Kinder has been named as the offensive backs coach at Davidson College.', 'Gerry Schof has been named as the defensive coordinator at Davidson College.', 'Dan Lloyd has been named as the head coach at Villanova University.', 'Stevie Njirgal has been named as the offensive ends coach at Davidson College.', 'Bill Campli has been named as the wide receiver coach at Rutgers University.', 'Mike Wade has been promoted to full-time status as the wide receiver coach at Missouri University.', 'John Rint has been named as the defensive coordinator at Delaware State University.', 'Ernie Altam** and **Doug Roman** have been named as the offensive line and defensive line coaches at Columbia University.', 'Lynn Hailstock has been named as the secondary coach at Cincinnati University.', 'Willie Burden has been named as the receivers coach at Ohio State University.', 'Mike Poe has been named as the head wrestling coach at Millikin University.', 'Jack Runchey and **Bernie Anderson** have been named as assistant coaches at Western Michigan University.', 'Paul Allen has resigned from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute to enter private business.', "Pete Carr has been hired as a men's soccer assistant coach at Drew University.", "Janae Nell has been named as the women's soccer assistant coach at Plymouth State University.", 'John Munns has been named as the head wrestling coach at George Washington University.', 'Mike Poe has been named as the head wrestling coach at Millikin University.', 'Rick Warner and **Wes Kubacki** have been named as assistant coaches at Hobart College.', 'Jack Streck has been hired as the head coach at Southeast Missouri State University.']), BatchSummary(summary="The text discusses several NCAA-related topics, including a basketball schedule for the 1983-84 season, compensation for graduate assistants in Division I sports, and a penalty imposed on New York Institute of Technology for violating NCAA rules. The basketball schedule includes games between various teams, with NBC-TV televising some of these games. The text also mentions the NCAA's Committee on Intercollegiate Athletics and its various subcommittees, as well as a penalty imposed on New York Institute of Technology for financial aid provided to student-athletes. The summary highlights these main points and provides context for each. ", key_facts=['NBC-TV will televise four college basketball games in the 1983-84 season, including a rematch of the 1982 national championship game between North Carolina State and Houston. ', "The NCAA's Compensation Committee has clarified the amount of compensation that can be received by graduate assistants in Division I sports, based on the normal stipend for graduate students enrolled in the specific graduate degree program. ", 'New York Institute of Technology has been placed on probation for one year by the NCAA for providing financial assistance to student-athletes without prior approval. ', "The NCAA's Committee on Intercollegiate Athletics has established various subcommittees to address concerns in higher education, including the Special Committee on Division I Criteria, the Special Committee on Officiating Improvement, and the Special Committee on Player Agents. "]), BatchSummary(summary="New Mexico State University has been approved to join the Pacific Coast Athletic Association for the entire 1983-1984 academic year. However, the Aggies will not compete for the football championship until the fall of 1984. The conference will sponsor six new championships in women's sports, including cross country, softball, swimming, tennis, and volleyball. The Trans America Athletic Conference will add men's volleyball and rifle to its conference championships in 1983-84, bringing the total number of men's championships to eight. Five Wisconsin universities have formed a men's soccer conference, which will begin play this fall. Mary T. Meagher, the 1983 NCAA Division I women's swimming champion, will not compete in intercollegiate competition in 1984 to prepare for the summer Olympics.", key_facts=['New Mexico State University approved to join the Pacific Coast Athletic Association for the entire 1983-1984 academic year', "Trans America Athletic Conference will add men's volleyball and rifle to its conference championships in 1983-84", "Five Wisconsin universities have formed a men's soccer conference", "Mary T. Meagher, the 1983 NCAA Division I women's swimming champion, will not compete in intercollegiate competition in 1984 to prepare for the summer Olympics"]), BatchSummary(summary='Michigun 1% Bo Schemhechler head.r Ihc arrive Divkion I-A list with 171 vrc~Iorirs./oliowed h.v Penn .yIulr :v Joe Pu~c~no (162) und Georgia :s Vine Lkml~ v (IS I). Women’s Gymnastics: $300 fines for non-compliance. Men’s Ice Hockey: Format approved for 1984 Division III championship. Women’s Softball: 1984 Division I Women’s Volleyball Championship requires 10% of games against other Division I teams. Wrestling: General round table eliminated, additional round tables for different divisions. Mid-American Conference basketball tournament alternates between Rockford, Illinois, and Toledo, Ohio. Wrestling Committee request for additional official denied, but permission granted to pay one current official 600 for that purpose. Executive Committee approved recommendation regarding sports polls. Executive Committee voted to consider using excess income to offset per diem expenses at championships in all divisions. James Shaffer named director of media services.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not mentioned', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not relevant. Studies focus on California red-legged frog and arroyo toad surveys on Los Padres National Forest, including terrestrial activity and conservation, amphibian decline factors, species boundaries, movement ecology, and pesticide impacts. Studies cover surveys, habitat use, population declines, and conservation efforts in California, including Kings Canyon National Park and Sequoia National Park. Research examines effects of dams, invasive species, and pesticides on amphibian populations, particularly red-legged frogs and mountain yellow-legged frogs. Conservation measures include safe harbor agreements and pesticide restrictions. Studies span from 1997 to 2014, covering multiple locations in California, including Los Padres National Forest, Kings Canyon National Park, and Sequoia National Park.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary="California red-legged frog (Rana draytonii) populations are threatened in southern California, with nine small, geographically isolated populations detected across four mountain ranges. The species is federally threatened and known to be easily detectable in a single visit during the frog's active season. Habitat use includes ephemeral and permanent water sources, natural and constructed wetlands, and lentic and lotic hydrology. Frogs move to nonbreeding areas, with some moving up to 1.4 km, and exhibit individual variation in departure rate from natal ponds. Threats include climate change, habitat loss, and introduced species. Conservation efforts focus on maintaining all essential habitat components and creating buffer zones around breeding, nonbreeding, and migration areas.", key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not relevant to the search result batch. Batch discusses declines and threats to California red-legged frogs and mountain yellow-legged frogs, including habitat loss, water diversion, cattle grazing, exotic aquatic species, climate change impacts, and disease (chytridiomycosis). Studies and reports mention declines in R. draytonii and R. muscosa populations, threats from non-native species, and the need for management and education to prevent further declines.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses declines of California red-legged frogs, including habitat alterations, competition with introduced species, and studies on site occupancy trends and factors affecting local extinction and colonization. Studies suggest negative impacts of non-native species such as bullfrogs and fish, but also indicate habitat alterations could contribute to declines. Research on northern red-legged frogs in Oregon and mountain yellow-legged frogs in Sierra Nevada, implicating pesticides and airborne pollutants in declines.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not mentioned. Studies on California red-legged frogs (Rana draytonii) and foothill yellow-legged frogs (Rana boylii) include concentration-response curves, SSDs, and risk curves for fish and aquatic invertebrates. Toxicity studies of chlorpyrifos and endosulfan to Pacific treefrogs (Pseudacris regilla) and foothill yellow-legged frogs (Rana boylii) are conducted. Population declines and internal range structure are observed in R. draytonii. Genetic studies show reduced diversity and differentiation in northern Sierra Nevada populations compared to coastal Bay Area populations. Pesticides from the Central Valley are implicated in population declines of R. muscosa and R. sierrae. Diet studies indicate preference for terrestrial prey in Foothill Yellow-Legged Frogs (Rana boylii) and American Bullfrogs (Lithobates catesbeianus). Conservation genetics suggest unique evolutionary lineages in R. muscosa populations. Effects of water temperature on breeding, growth, and metamorphosis of Foothill Yellow-Legged Frogs (Rana boylii) are studied in regulated and unregulated riverine environments. Sensitive species of snakes, frogs, and salamanders in southern California conifer forest areas are identified and managed. Highly and low pathogenic avian influenza virus infection in red-legged partridge (Alectoris rufa) is investigated for pathogenesis and transmissibility.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found in the provided search result batch. Batch contains studies on avian influenza in red-legged partridges, pox infection in a rough-legged hawk, effects of lead shot ingestion on red-legged partridge bone mineralization, multiple stressors and amphibian declines, and an outbreak of encephalitic listeriosis in red-legged partridges. No information relevant to Amream LLC was found.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not relevant. Studies focus on California red-legged frogs, West Nile virus in partridges, airborne pesticides impact, mercury contamination, and agricultural runoff effects on amphibians in California and British Columbia. Studies conducted in 2000-2007 and 2002. Mercury contamination found in water, sediment, and biota from historical gold mining. Agricultural runoff affects hatching success and deformity rates of native amphibians in British Columbia. West Nile virus infection in red-legged partridges shows moderate virulence and immune response in brain. Checkerboard dredging pattern for California red-legged frogs reduces impacts from airborne pesticides. Astaxanthin and papilioerythrinone identified in red-legged partridge skin. Effects of red light cameras on motorcycle crashes at signalized intersections identified. Mercury contamination in water, sediment, and biota from mining activities in Sierra Nevada, California. Hatching success and deformity rates of amphibians affected by agricultural runoff in Sumas Prairie, British Columbia.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not mentioned. Studies on red-legged frogs in Lower Fraser Valley, Canada, suggest agricultural runoff affects reproductive success. Red-legged partridges have no significant differences in haematocrit, plasma glucose, or creatine kinase activity with age or sex. Kittiwakes in Alaska exhibit poor productivity since mid-1970s, with colony failures exceeding 50% in 1985-1989. African clawed frogs in California exhibit differences in growth and fatbody cycles. Red-legged partridge anti-predator behavior is improved by training with parent partridges. Carotenoid-based bill and eye ring coloration in red-legged partridges is associated with body condition and stress. Rough-legged buzzards, Arctic foxes, and red foxes in a tundra ecosystem without rodents exhibit different breeding dynamics.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses anurans in California, including red-legged frogs, tree frogs, and toads. Studies on crash models for rural intersections, absence of protection from West Nile virus in red-legged partridges, use of arboreal nests by amphibians, behavioral responses to predators, sea birds as proxies for marine habitats, effects of ammonium sulfate on salamanders and tadpoles, and contaminant studies in Sierra Nevadas. No information relevant to Amream LLC found.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses California red-legged frog, Furadan 3G impacts, acetylcholinesterase activity, vancomycin-resistant enterococci in wild animals, Oregon spotted frog surveys, virulence of Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis (Bd) in Cascades frogs, and contact toxicities of anuran skin alkaloids against fire ants. Salmonella isolates from wild amphibians and reptiles in California are also discussed.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found in the provided content. Content discusses biotic resistance, amphibian health, reproduction, hibernation, carotenoid-based ornaments, parasite infection, seabird population trends, and internal pigmentation in frogs. No information relevant to Amream LLC is present.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content unrelated to Amream LLC includes studies on wildlife consumption in Congo, growth classification systems for red fir and white fir in California, population trends and monitoring of Rana draytonii (California Red-legged Frog), integration of occupancy and structural equation models for species occurrence, parasites of African clawed frogs in California, peptidomic analysis of tailed frogs, grapevine red blotch-associated virus in California vineyards, competitive pressures affecting frog vocal complexity, CRF-like peptide from Phyllomedusa hypochondrialis, and muscle activation during one-legged bridge exercise with sling and hip abduction.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amphibian diseases not presented include chlamydia and mycobacteria infections in captive African clawed frogs and northern leopard frogs. Helminthic parasites of amphibians are generally innocuous, except for trematode Ribeiroia linked to malformations. Iridoviruses naturally infecting different taxonomic classes are isolated from wild sympatric fish and amphibians. Pesticides and urbanization are implicated in amphibian population declines. Red band needle blight affects hybrid Pinus attenuata X P. radiata. Genetic data suggest conservation planning should consider rivers and hydrologic regions for foothill yellow-legged frogs. Parathion accumulation in cricket frogs affects American kestrels. Joint kinetics and kinematics differ among common lower limb rehabilitation exercises. Red fir health in upper montane California forests is assessed. Grapevine red blotch virus is transmitted by Spissistilus festinus. OMB control numbers for certain agricultural products are listed.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses barking frogs (Eleutherodactylus augusti) in Arizona, including their distribution, call structure, and genetic differences among populations. Studies also cover dermaseptin, adenoregulin, and caerin peptides from the Central American red-eyed leaf frog, Agalychnis callidryas. Additionally, a study compares joint kinetics and kinematics during rehabilitation exercises, and another discusses the growth of California red fir after overstory removal and thinning. Lastly, a paper presents preliminary results on a powered lower limb orthosis for paraplegic individuals.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses red-legged frogs, Barking Frogs, prey selection in red spotted green frogs, balance and locomotion in a one-legged hopping machine, dietary restriction effects on corticosterone levels in red-legged kittiwake chicks, prefrontal oxygenation and ventilatory responses during one-legged cycling exercise, efficacy of ivermectin as an anthelmintic in leopard frogs, active capsule endoscopy, heart rots in California red fir and white fir, population density of goldspotted oak borer in California and Arizona oaks, and growth modeling of brown frogs.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found in the provided search result batch. Batch contains studies on genetic structure of frogs in southern China and Indochina, anurophagy in frogs, and red light violation data in California. No information relevant to Amream LLC.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses red-legged frogs, Douglas-fir, red fir, and other ecological studies in California and United States.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content discusses California red-legged frogs, grapevine red blotch disease, California black oak, door-opening method for six-legged robots, tailed frog movements, spring rail frogs, radiocesium contamination in frogs, dietary alkaloid sequestration in poison frogs, adaptive interlimb coordination in legged robots, Ternus effect, red light camera enforcement impact, and chytridiomycosis in Asian frogs. No information relevant to Amream LLC found.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary='Amream LLC not found. Content unrelated to Amream LLC includes studies on red-legged frogs, parasitic infections, and tick-borne pathogens in California. Research on red-legged frog pheromones, habitat selection, and tick-borne diseases are presented without mentioning Amream LLC.', key_facts=[]), BatchSummary(summary="Amream LLC not mentioned. California red-legged frogs are found 